# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# fukuii-cli — .gitignore
#
# This repository is PUBLIC. This file is the gate between what stays local and
# what the world sees. Never weaken a SECURITY block below; add repo-specific
# ignores under the marked section at the bottom.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# == SECURITY: secrets & credentials ==
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.*.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
id_rsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
credentials.json
service-account.json
*.credentials
secrets/
.secrets/
*.secret

# == SECURITY: package-registry auth ==
# These carry registry auth tokens (`_authToken`, `npmAuthIdent`). A committed
# .npmrc can also silently weaken install-time supply-chain settings.
.npmrc
.yarnrc
.yarnrc.yml

# == SECURITY: developer-environment credentials ==
# `.envrc` is the trap here: direnv's file is routinely `export AWS_SECRET…`, and
# `.env.*` above LOOKS like it covers it but does not — there is no dot before
# `rc`. The rest are shell and SSH credential conventions with no extension of
# their own, so no glob above reaches them.
.envrc
.netrc
_netrc
.git-credentials
*.p8
*.ppk
*_rsa
*_ecdsa
*_ed25519
deploy_key

# == SECURITY: node / chain key material ==
# Match the ARTIFACT, not the directory it happens to sit in. A node writes these
# under whatever --datadir it is given, so any pattern anchored to the repo root
# protects exactly one datadir choice out of many.
#
# This block was root-anchored throughout until an adversarial review reproduced
# the gap: with `--datadir ./data`, a `git add -A` staged an encrypted v3 keystore
# and a plaintext BIP-39 mnemonic. On a public repo that is unrecoverable — the
# blob stays fetchable after `git rm --cached`, so the remedy would be key
# rotation, not removal.
#
# The one anchoring that IS load-bearing is the keystore DIRECTORY. Unanchored,
# `keystore/` swallows the `org.fukuii.…keystore` SOURCE PACKAGE (KeyStore, Wallet,
# EncryptedKey, EncryptedKeyJsonCodec + two specs were tracked source in the
# pre-rebuild tree). That collision is real for a directory name and CANNOT exist
# for a filename: no Scala package is ever named `wallet.json`. Anchoring those
# was pattern-copying, not a collision test.
#
# `UTC--*` is what makes the directory anchoring safe to keep: it is the geth and
# mantis keystore filename convention, no source file has that shape, so it closes
# every nested keystore at any depth with no collision. Verified by effect in both
# directions — nested datadir artifacts ignored, keystore source package visible.
# Do not re-anchor these without re-running that check.
UTC--*
/keystore/
wallet.json
mnemonic.txt
jwt.hex
jwtsecret
*.nodekey
# node.key is already covered globally by `*.key` above; kept for intent.
node.key
# The bare, extensionless `nodekey` is the geth-family filename — go-ethereum and
# core-geth write the node's secp256k1 private key to `<datadir>/geth/nodekey`.
# It matches NONE of the three patterns above: `*.nodekey` and `*.key` both
# require a dot, and `node.key` is a different name. So the actual filename this
# repository's own reference clients produce was the one uncovered.
#
# Found the way the rest of this block was found — by adversarial review, and
# settled by effect after two agents disagreed about whether it was already
# covered. One reported it covered; it was not. That is the dangerous direction,
# and it is why this block verifies rather than reads its own patterns.
#
# Unanchored deliberately, per the datadir reasoning above: `--datadir ./data`
# puts it at `data/geth/nodekey`, not at the root. No collision is possible with
# tracked source — a Scala file always carries an extension.
nodekey

# == SECURITY: infra / cloud ==
# No `!*.tfvars.example` carve-out: `*.tfvars` never matches a name ending
# `.example`, so the negation was inert. Proven by differential — deleting it
# changed nothing. A negation that cannot change an outcome is worse than absent,
# because a test written against it passes regardless of this file's contents.
*.tfvars
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
.terraform/
*.kubeconfig
kubeconfig
.pgpass
docker-compose.override.yml

# == Local working files — never travel ==
.local/
SESSION_NOTES.md
PLANNING.md
SCRATCH.md
*.scratch.*
*.draft.*

# == Claude Code ==
# This directory is TRACKED so repo-local agents, skills and rules travel with the
# repo. Only machine-local and agent-written state is held back. Do not invert this
# into a blanket ignore: an agent inside an ignored directory never reaches a clone.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/worktrees/
.claude/agent-memory-local/
.claude/launch.json
.claude/scheduled_tasks.json
CLAUDE.local.md
.claude/**/*.local.*
.claude/**/*-local/

# == OS ==
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# == Editors / IDE ==
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.metals/
.bloop/
.bsp/
metals.sbt

# == Logs / temp ==
*.log
*.log.*
/logs/
/tmp/
*.tmp
*.pid

# == JVM / Scala / sbt ==
# `/out/` is root-anchored: unanchored it would hide any `src/main/resources/out/`
# a module ships. sbt builds to `target/`; `out/` is Mill's convention and Mill was
# assessed and rejected, so the root entry is a courtesy, not a live build path.
target/
/out/
project/boot/
project/plugins/project/
project/project/
project/metals.sbt
*.class
*.sc

# == Node / JS / TS ==
node_modules/
/dist/
/build/
/coverage/
*.tsbuildinfo
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches

# == Python ==
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# == Docs build ==
/site/
/_site/

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Repo-specific ignores BELOW this line (safe to edit per repo):
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# A mutated copy of the certification gate, written by check-test-run-proof.sh
# with its guard disabled. Removed on exit; a SIGKILL mid-proof leaves it behind
# inside a tracked directory, and it must never be committed.
scripts/.proof-mutant.sh
